CHUNK [120 bytes]: "To support a real-time feedback loop that allows the AI to \"learn\" from user votes and apply that logic across a series,"
CHUNK [131 bytes]: " the architecture must move away from static processing and toward a **Dynamic Prompting & Vector-Augmented Retrieval (RAG)** model"
CHUNK [101 bytes]: ".\n\nBecause we cannot re-train a model every time a user clicks \"Upvote,\" we use **In-Context Learning"
CHUNK [100 bytes]: "**.\n\n### The Real-Time Feedback Loop Architecture\n\n#### 1. The Component Stack\n*   **Vector Database"
CHUNK [90 bytes]: " (e.g., Pinecone or Weaviate):** Stores the \"Project Profile.\" It holds embeddings of text"
CHUNK [98 bytes]: " snippets the user has interacted with, tagged as \"Confirmed Trope\" or \"False Positive.\"\n*   **Rel"
CHUNK [112 bytes]: "ational Database (PostgreSQL):** Stores the hard metadata (User IDs, Project Settings, Annotations).\n*   **Orche"
CHUNK [119 bytes]: "stration Layer (LangChain or Semantic Kernel):** Manages the \"Context Construction\" before sending a request to the LLM"
CHUNK [112 bytes]: ".\n*   **Feedback Cache (Redis):** Stores the active project’s \"Negative Constraints\" for low-latency retrieval"
CHUNK [82 bytes]: " during real-time analysis.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. The Data Flow (The \"Vote\" Cycle)\n\n**Step"
CHUNK [87 bytes]: " A: The User Signal**\n1.  User clicks **Downvote** on a trope card (\"The Reluctant Hero"
CHUNK [72 bytes]: "\") in Book 1.\n2.  The Frontend sends a payload: `{ProjectID, BookID, Sni"
CHUNK [70 bytes]: "ppetID, TropeID, Vote: -1, VectorID}`.\n\n**Step B: Updating the Project"
CHUNK [94 bytes]: " Profile**\n1.  The Backend saves the vote to PostgreSQL for the audit trail.\n2.  The **Snippet"
CHUNK [110 bytes]: "** and its **Trope Definition** are sent to the Vector DB. This creates a \"Negative Example\" for this specific"
CHUNK [109 bytes]: " project.\n3.  The **Project Profile** is updated with a summary instruction: *\"User rejected 'Reluctant Hero'"
CHUNK [106 bytes]: " when applied to Character 'John' in scenes involving dialogue.\"*\n\n**Step C: The Real-Time Adjustment (The"
CHUNK [104 bytes]: " Loop)**\nWhen the user moves to the next chapter or Book 2:\n1.  **Semantic Retrieval:** Before analyzing"
CHUNK [133 bytes]: " a new paragraph, the system queries the Vector DB: *\"Is this new paragraph similar to any snippets this user previously rejected?\"*\n"
CHUNK [110 bytes]: "2.  **Prompt Injection (Dynamic Context):** The LLM is not just asked \"Find tropes.\" The prompt is dynamically"
CHUNK [90 bytes]: " built:\n    *   *Instruction:* \"Analyze this text for tropes.\"\n    *   *User Preferences:*"
CHUNK [122 bytes]: " \"The user in this project specifically excludes 'The Reluctant Hero' for the character John.\" (Injected from the Feedback"
CHUNK [95 bytes]: " Cache).\n    *   *Examples:* \"Here is an example of what this user considers a 'False Positive'"
CHUNK [100 bytes]: " in this series: [Snippet].\"\n3.  **Inference:** The LLM produces a more accurate result based on the"
CHUNK [105 bytes]: " specific \"Project Profile.\"\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Handling Cross-Volume Verification (The \"Conflict Engine\")\n\nTo"
CHUNK [129 bytes]: " prevent the AI from becoming too \"brave\" and overriding user settings without permission, we implement a **Validation Gateway**:"
CHUNK [100 bytes]: "\n\n1.  **Pre-check:** When Book 2 is uploaded, the system does a batch vector search of all potential"
CHUNK [92 bytes]: " tropes against the Project Profile's \"Downvoted\" list.\n2.  **Conflict Flagging:** If the AI"
CHUNK [125 bytes]: " finds a high-probability trope that matches a previous \"Negative Example,\" it does not hide it immediately. It marks it with"
CHUNK [81 bytes]: " a `status: \"CONFLICT\"`.\n3.  **UI Notification:** The \"Conflict Center\" in the UI"
CHUNK [121 bytes]: " polls the API for any items with a conflict status and presents them to the user: *\"You rejected this pattern in Book 1;"
CHUNK [88 bytes]: " is it valid here?\"*\n\n---\n\n#### 4. The Processing Pipeline (Real-Time vs. Background)\n\n|"
CHUNK [74 bytes]: " Feature | Tier 1: Real-Time (<10k words) | Tier 2: Background (>10k words"
CHUNK [72 bytes]: ") |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Logic** | Streaming LLM response. | Batch"
CHUNK [86 bytes]: " job via Celery/Redis. |\n| **Context** | Injects the top 5 most recent user votes into"
CHUNK [97 bytes]: " the prompt. | Injects the full \"Project Profile\" summary. |\n| **UI** | Dynamic highlights appear"
CHUNK [106 bytes]: " as the user reads. | Analysis \"Heatmap\" populates once the job finishes. |\n| **Verification** | Conflicts"
CHUNK [106 bytes]: " are highlighted in a \"Review\" sidebar. | Conflicts are compiled into a \"Consistency Report.\" |\n\n---\n\n####"
CHUNK [75 bytes]: " 5. Why this works for an MVP\n*   **No Re-training:** Fine-tuning an LLM on"
CHUNK [104 bytes]: " user votes is expensive and slow. This \"Dynamic Prompting\" (RAG) approach happens in milliseconds.\n*   "
CHUNK [137 bytes]: "**Scalability:** By using a Vector DB, the system can compare a single paragraph against thousands of previous user votes instantly.\n*   "
CHUNK [110 bytes]: "**Accuracy:** Using BookNLP's **Quotation Attribution** ensures the feedback is surgically precise (e.g., \"Don"
CHUNK [109 bytes]: "'t find this trope for *this* character's dialogue\").\n\n### Next Steps Recommendation\nTo build this, the first"
CHUNK [116 bytes]: " technical milestone should be the **Database Schema** that links **BookNLP Entities** (Characters/Places) to **User"
CHUNK [109 bytes]: " Votes**. \n\n**Would you like to see the proposed Database Schema to see how these relationships are stored?**"
